1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Right now you're probably in a room at

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:04,880
about twenty-two degrees, and your body is losing

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,960
heat to it every second.

4
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:10,240
Yet your core temperature stays close to thirty-seven.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:13,600
Something keeps detecting that drift and correcting it.

6
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:16,400
So what detects it, what decides, and what

7
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:17,360
does the fixing?

8
00:00:18,567 --> 00:00:22,886
That steadiness has a name: homeostasis, keeping the

9
00:00:22,967 --> 00:00:27,366
internal environment relatively stable while the outside changes.

10
00:00:27,687 --> 00:00:29,526
Relatively is the key word.

11
00:00:30,087 --> 00:00:33,206
Core temperature wobbles within a narrow band, roughly

12
00:00:33,367 --> 00:00:35,526
thirty-six and a half to thirty-seven and a

13
00:00:35,607 --> 00:00:36,486
half degrees.

14
00:00:36,967 --> 00:00:39,686
We call that band the tolerance range, and

15
00:00:39,767 --> 00:00:41,926
the value it wobbles around is the set

16
00:00:42,007 --> 00:00:42,646
point.

17
00:00:42,967 --> 00:00:43,766
Why does it matter?

18
00:00:44,247 --> 00:00:46,806
Your enzymes work well only in a narrow

19
00:00:46,887 --> 00:00:48,126
band of conditions.

20
00:00:48,327 --> 00:00:50,246
Too hot, and they can start losing their

21
00:00:50,327 --> 00:00:50,726
shape.

22
00:00:51,207 --> 00:00:53,766
Too cold, and reactions slow down.

23
00:00:54,967 --> 00:00:57,126
A heater with a thermostat works the same

24
00:00:57,207 --> 00:00:57,606
way.

25
00:00:58,087 --> 00:01:00,566
You set the dial to twenty degrees: that's

26
00:01:00,607 --> 00:01:01,366
the set point.

27
00:01:01,847 --> 00:01:04,006
A sensor inside measures the room.

28
00:01:04,407 --> 00:01:06,806
A switch compares the reading with the dial.

29
00:01:07,367 --> 00:01:09,766
When the room drops below twenty, the switch

30
00:01:09,847 --> 00:01:10,966
turns the heater on.

31
00:01:11,527 --> 00:01:13,526
As the room warms back up, the switch

32
00:01:13,607 --> 00:01:14,566
turns it off again.

33
00:01:15,127 --> 00:01:17,526
Detect, decide, act.

34
00:01:18,734 --> 00:01:21,053
Your body runs the same kind of loop,

35
00:01:21,134 --> 00:01:22,413
with five parts.

36
00:01:22,734 --> 00:01:24,853
Say you step into a cold wind.

37
00:01:25,134 --> 00:01:28,573
First, the stimulus: a change in the variable,

38
00:01:28,734 --> 00:01:31,333
here body temperature starting to fall.

39
00:01:31,614 --> 00:01:36,253
Second, a receptor detects it: thermoreceptors, temperature sensors

40
00:01:36,294 --> 00:01:38,733
in your skin and in the hypothalamus, a

41
00:01:38,894 --> 00:01:40,973
small region at the base of the brain.

42
00:01:41,174 --> 00:01:44,813
Third, the control centre, the hypothalamus, compares that

43
00:01:44,894 --> 00:01:46,733
signal with the set point.

44
00:01:46,814 --> 00:01:50,093
Fourth, it signals effectors, the muscles and glands

45
00:01:50,174 --> 00:01:51,133
that act.

46
00:01:51,174 --> 00:01:54,173
Skeletal muscles start shivering, and small arteries in

47
00:01:54,214 --> 00:01:57,133
the skin, called arterioles, narrow.

48
00:01:57,174 --> 00:02:00,493
Fifth, the response: more heat is made, less

49
00:02:00,574 --> 00:02:02,893
is lost, and temperature climbs back.

50
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:05,300
Here's the key idea.

51
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:08,740
The response pushes the variable the opposite way

52
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:10,180
to the original change.

53
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:13,620
Temperature fell, so the response raises it.

54
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:17,460
That's why it's called negative feedback: negative means

55
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:19,140
opposing, not bad.

56
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:21,140
And watch what happens next.

57
00:02:21,620 --> 00:02:25,460
As temperature returns, the receptors detect less change,

58
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:28,100
so their signals weaken and the extra shivering

59
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:28,820
fades.

60
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:31,940
The response settles to what's needed, not to

61
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:34,820
zero: in a cold room, some extra heat

62
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:37,540
production carries on, because the cold hasn't gone

63
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:38,340
away.

64
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:40,820
And small delays can let temperature drift a

65
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:42,980
little either side of the set point.

66
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:47,320
Compare that with positive feedback, where the response

67
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:49,480
strengthens the original change.

68
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:52,360
When a blood vessel tears, a few platelets

69
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:53,800
stick to the damaged wall.

70
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:57,000
They release chemicals that call in more platelets,

71
00:02:57,080 --> 00:02:58,520
which release more chemicals.

72
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,640
The change grows until the hole is plugged,

73
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:02,920
and then it stops.

74
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:05,880
Positive feedback drives a process to an end

75
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:06,680
point.

76
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:10,120
It can't hold a variable steady, so homeostasis

77
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:12,120
relies on negative feedback.

78
00:03:13,334 --> 00:03:16,373
The same five-part template fits other variables.

79
00:03:16,854 --> 00:03:19,173
Blood glucose rises after a meal.

80
00:03:19,654 --> 00:03:21,893
Cells in the pancreas detect the rise and

81
00:03:22,054 --> 00:03:23,733
release a hormone, insulin.

82
00:03:24,294 --> 00:03:27,573
Body cells, especially liver and muscle cells, take

83
00:03:27,654 --> 00:03:30,853
up more glucose, so blood glucose falls back.

84
00:03:31,494 --> 00:03:34,293
Here, the pancreas is both the receptor and

85
00:03:34,374 --> 00:03:35,413
the control centre.

86
00:03:35,894 --> 00:03:38,133
You'll see how insulin and its partner hormone

87
00:03:38,214 --> 00:03:39,733
work in a later video.

88
00:03:40,934 --> 00:03:42,933
When you read a loop in an exam,

89
00:03:43,094 --> 00:03:44,293
check two things.

90
00:03:44,534 --> 00:03:47,773
First, which variable is being regulated?

91
00:03:48,054 --> 00:03:51,453
In homeostasis, it's a condition inside the body.

92
00:03:51,734 --> 00:03:54,053
Air temperature might cause the change, but your

93
00:03:54,134 --> 00:03:56,573
body isn't trying to control the air.

94
00:03:57,094 --> 00:04:00,133
Second, which way does the response push?

95
00:04:00,214 --> 00:04:03,493
If it opposes the change, it's negative feedback.

96
00:04:03,574 --> 00:04:06,573
And remember, receptors detect the change in the

97
00:04:06,854 --> 00:04:08,453
variable, not the response.

98
00:04:09,667 --> 00:04:12,946
To remember the order: Some Really Cool Elephants

99
00:04:13,027 --> 00:04:13,426
Run.

100
00:04:13,907 --> 00:04:18,466
Stimulus, receptor, control centre, effector, response.

101
00:04:18,947 --> 00:04:21,506
And if the response opposes the change, the

102
00:04:21,587 --> 00:04:22,626
loop is negative.

103
00:04:23,834 --> 00:04:25,193
Two exam questions.

104
00:04:25,674 --> 00:04:26,153
First.

105
00:04:26,714 --> 00:04:30,153
During exercise, blood carbon dioxide rises.

106
00:04:30,714 --> 00:04:33,913
Chemoreceptors in the brainstem and major arteries detect

107
00:04:33,994 --> 00:04:34,633
the rise.

108
00:04:35,194 --> 00:04:37,993
The medulla oblongata sends nerve impulses to the

109
00:04:38,074 --> 00:04:40,873
diaphragm and the muscles between the ribs, and

110
00:04:40,954 --> 00:04:43,113
breathing becomes faster and deeper.

111
00:04:43,754 --> 00:04:46,633
Construct a negative feedback loop for this response,

112
00:04:46,714 --> 00:04:48,953
and explain why it's negative feedback.

113
00:04:49,514 --> 00:04:50,793
Pause here to answer.

114
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:04,560
Stimulus: blood carbon dioxide rises.

115
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:07,200
Receptor: the chemoreceptors.

116
00:05:07,440 --> 00:05:10,320
Control centre: the medulla oblongata.

117
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:13,760
Effectors: the diaphragm and rib muscles.

118
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,560
Response: faster, deeper breathing removes more carbon dioxide,

119
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:19,840
so its level falls.

120
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:23,680
It's negative because the response opposes the rise.

121
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,680
Calling exercise the stimulus is the classic mistake:

122
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:30,720
the stimulus is the change in carbon dioxide.

123
00:05:31,934 --> 00:05:32,493
Second.

124
00:05:32,974 --> 00:05:34,973
A healthy adult moved from a room at

125
00:05:35,054 --> 00:05:38,173
twenty-two degrees into a room at five degrees.

126
00:05:38,814 --> 00:05:41,293
The graph shows air temperature and the adult's

127
00:05:41,414 --> 00:05:42,573
core body temperature.

128
00:05:43,214 --> 00:05:46,413
Identify the variable being regulated, and explain the

129
00:05:46,494 --> 00:05:49,453
pattern in core body temperature after the move,

130
00:05:49,534 --> 00:05:51,373
with reference to negative feedback.

131
00:05:52,014 --> 00:05:53,373
Pause here to answer.

132
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:07,160
The regulated variable is core body temperature, not

133
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:08,120
air temperature.

134
00:06:08,680 --> 00:06:10,720
Its small fall after the move is the

135
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:11,480
stimulus.

136
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:15,160
The hypothalamus responded with shivering and narrower skin

137
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:18,680
arterioles, which made more heat and lost less,

138
00:06:18,760 --> 00:06:20,360
so core temperature recovered.

139
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,800
Naming air temperature as the regulated variable is

140
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:26,920
the classic mistake: the air stayed at five

141
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:27,800
degrees.

142
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,880
So for any loop, name the variable inside

143
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:34,760
the body, trace the five parts in order,

144
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:37,960
and check that the response opposes the change.

145
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:39,480
Next, we'll zoom in on

146
00:06:40,040 --> 00:06:43,240
temperature: how endotherms detect heat, and the main

147
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:45,000
ways they lose or keep it.

148
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:47,640
Here are the key notes.

149
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:49,800
Pause the video if you need more time

150
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:50,680
to copy them.
